iction by the same\n   argument (the hole \\((j,\\pi(j))\\) would be forced inside \\(R\\)).\n\n3. **One point is \\(A_i\\) and the other is \\(B_j\\).**  \n   If \\(i\\le j\\) then the rows of the two points are \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\); the\n   column interval contains \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\).  \n   Because \\(i\\le j\\), the row \\(i+1\\) lies between \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\); the\n   column \\(\\pi(i+1)\\) lies in the column interval, so the hole\n   \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\) belongs to \\(R\\), a contradiction.  \n   The case \\(i>j\\) is analogous, producing the hole \\((j+1,\\pi(j+1))\\).\n\nThus any rectangle avoiding all holes can contain at most one element of\n\\(\\mathcal{C}\\). ∎\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) has exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) elements.  \nSince each rectangle can contain at most one of them, any tiling of the\nboard minus the holes needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles }\\ge 2n-2 } \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nDefine, for \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid k+1\\le r\\le n,\\; 1\\le c\\le k\\},\n\\qquad\nS_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k,\\; k+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n* Each \\(R_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\); it lies entirely\n  below the diagonal, hence contains no hole.\n* Each \\(S_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\); it lies entirely\n  above the diagonal, hence contains no hole.\n* The family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{S_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise\n  disjoint and its union is precisely the set of all cells that are not on\n  the diagonal.\n\nTherefore the board, after removing the diagonal holes, is tiled with\n\\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles }\\le 2n