(n-1\\) rectangles, and \\(n-1\\) rectangles are sufficient.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - An **inner corner** of \\(Y\\) is a cell \\((r,c)\\) such that \\((r,c)\\in Y\\), \\((r+1,c)\\notin Y\\) and \\((r,c+1)\\in Y\\). Because the row lengths are distinct, there are exactly \\(n-1\\) inner corners, one for each transition where the row length increases.  \n   - A rectangle contained in \\(Y\\) can contain **at most one** inner corner: if it contained two distinct inner corners \\((r_1,c_1)\\) and \\((r_2,c_2)\\) with \\(r_1<r_2\\) and \\(c_1<c_2\\), then the rectangle would also contain the cell \\((r_1,c_2)\\), which is absent from \\(Y\\) by definition of an inner corner\u2014a contradiction.  \n   - Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed.\n\n   - To achieve the bound, take the vertical strip of width\u202f1 for each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\). In a diagram with distinct row lengths the set of rows that contain column \\(c\\) forms a contiguous block, so each such strip is a rectangle. These \\(n-1\\) strips cover all cells of \\(Y\\). \u220e\n\n5. **Application of the lemma.**  \n   For our left region \\(L\\) we have \\(n=2025\\); therefore  \n   \\[\n   T_L\\ge n-1=2024.\n   \\]\n   The same argument applied to the right region gives  \n   \\[\n   T_R\\ge 2024.\n   \\]\n   Consequently  \n   \\[\n   T_{\\text{total}}\\ge 2024+2024=4048.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered cells lie on the main diagonal).  \n\n   - *Left region.* For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le 2024\\) the cells \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i>c\\) are exactly those left of the diagonal in that column; they form a contiguous vertical block of height \\(2025-c\\). Cover each such block by one rectangle of size \\((2025-c)\\times1\\). This uses \\(2024\\) rectangles.  \n\n   - *Right region.* Symmetrically, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le 2025\\) the cells \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i<c\\) are the cells right of the diagonal in that column; they form a contiguous block of height \\(c-1\\). Cover each by one rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\). Again we obtain \\(2024\\) rectangles.\n\n   The two families of rectangles are disjoint, cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell). The total number of tiles is \\(2024+2024=4048\\).\n\nSince the lower bound matches an explicit construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\pi(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Tiles cannot cross the \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 of uncovered cells  \n\nConsider any axis\u2011parallel rectangle \\(Q\\) placed on the board.  \nIf \\(Q\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) (i.e. a cell of the left side) and also a cell \\((i',j')\\) with \\(j'>\\pi(i')\\) (a cell of the right side), then the column interval of \\(Q\\) contains both a column smaller than \\(\\pi(i)\\) and a column larger than \\(\\pi(i')\\). Since the column interval of a rectangle is contiguous, it must contain the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) for the row \\(i\\). Hence the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would be covered, contradicting the requirement that uncovered cells remain uncovered.  \n\nTherefore **every tile lies entirely inside the set**\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad R=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\nConsequently the total number of tiles equals\n\\[\nT_{\\text{total}}=T_L+T_R,\n\\]\nwhere \\(T_L\\) (resp. \\(T_R\\)) is the minimum number of rectangles needed to tile \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  The left region is a Young diagram with distinct row lengths  \n\nReorder the rows so that the values \\(\\pi(i)\\) become increasing.  \nFormally, let \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma(1))<\\pi(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <\\pi(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nDefine the reordered rows by \\(i'=\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIn this order the left region consists, in row \\(i\\), of the first\n\\[\na_i=\\pi(\\sigma(i))-1\n\\]\ncells. Because \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_n\\}\\) equals\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus the row lengths are pairwise distinct.  \nHence \\(L\\) is a Young diagram (Ferrers diagram) whose row\u2011lengths are\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\nExactly the same description holds for the right region \\(R\\) after ordering the rows by decreasing \\(\\pi\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma on covering a distinct\u2011length Young diagram  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(Y\\) be a Young diagram with \\(n\\) rows whose row\u2011lengths are pairwise distinct. Any partition of \\(Y\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, and \\(n-1\\) rectangles suffice.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the row lengths are distinct, as we move from the top row to the bottom row the length increases at exactly \\(n-1\\) positions. At each such increase there is an **inner corner**: a cell \\((r,c)\\) belonging to \\(Y\\) such that the cell directly below it \\((r+1,c)\\) is absent while the cell to its right \\((r,c+1)\\) is present. There are precisely \\(n-1\\) inner corners.\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq Y\\). Suppose \\(Q\\) contained two distinct inner corners \\((r_1,c_1)\\) and \\((r_2,c_2)\\) with \\(r_1<r_2\\) and \\(c_1<c_2\\). Since \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, it would also contain the cell \\((r_1,c_2)\\). By definition of an inner corner, \\((r_1,c_2)\\) does **not** belong to \\(Y\\), a contradiction. Hence a rectangle can contain at most one inner corner.\n\nTherefore at least one rectangle is needed for each inner corner, i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nTo achieve the bound, take for each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) the vertical strip consisting of all rows whose length is at least \\(c\\). Because the row lengths are distinct, these rows form a contiguous block; the strip is a rectangle of width\u202f1. The \\(n-1\\) such strips cover every cell of \\(Y\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the original problem  \n\nApplying the lemma to the left region \\(L\\) (which has \\(n\\) rows with distinct lengths) yields\n\\[\nT_L\\ge n-1=2024.\n\\]\nApplying the same argument to the right region \\(R\\) gives\n\\[\nT_R\\ge 2024.\n\\]\nHence any admissible tiling satisfies\n\\[\nT_{\\text{total}}=T_L+T_R\\ge 2024+2024=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left side.* For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) the cells left of the diagonal in that column are\n\\[\n\\{(i,c): i>c\\}.\n\\]\nThese cells form a vertical block of height \\(n-c\\). Cover this block with a single rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\). Doing this for all \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) uses exactly \\(n-1=2024\\) rectangles.\n\n*Right side.* Symmetrically, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the cells right of the diagonal are\n\\[\n\\{(i,c): i<c\\},\n\\]\na vertical block of height \\(c-1\\). Cover each block with one rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\). This again uses \\(n-1=2024\\) rectangles.\n\nThe two families of rectangles are disjoint, together cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell). The total number of tiles is\n\\[\n2024+2024=4048.\n\\]\n\nSince the lower bound equals the number of tiles in this explicit tiling, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
